"# Stripe Audit Report

**Score: 0 / 100 — Grade F**

Stripe API \`2026-06-24.dahlia\` · account \`live\` · 2026-06-24T00:00:00.000Z

## Summary

| Severity | Count |
| --- | --- |
| 🔴 Critical | 1 |
| 🟠 High | 8 |
| 🟡 Medium | 15 |
| 🔵 Low | 6 |
| ⚪ Info | 2 |
| **Total** | **32** |

_Rules run: 35 · passed: 4 · suppressed: 0_

_Full audit — every region scanned within limits._

## Findings

### 🔴 Critical (1)

#### Webhook endpoint subscribes to all events (*)

Enabled endpoint https://star.example.com/hook subscribes to all event types (\`*\`). A blanket subscription hides which revenue-critical events are actually consumed, so a silently dropped \`invoice.payment_failed\` looks identical to an event you never wanted.

_Impact: Masked delivery failures of billing events can silently leak recurring revenue._

**Fix:** Subscribe each endpoint to the explicit list of events it handles. Reserve \`*\` for debugging, never production billing.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks) · rule \`WEBHOOK_SELECT_ALL\`

### 🟠 High (8)

#### Webhook endpoint is disabled

Endpoint https://a.example.com/h is disabled, so every event it subscribes to is currently dropped. Stripe disables endpoints automatically after repeated delivery failures.

**Fix:** Re-enable the endpoint once the receiver is healthy, or delete it if it is obsolete so it no longer reads as a coverage gap.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks/best-practices) · rule \`WEBHOOK_ENDPOINT_DISABLED\`

#### Insecure (non-HTTPS) webhook URL

Endpoint http://insecure.example.com/hook uses a non-HTTPS scheme on a live account. Event payloads (including signing material) travel unencrypted and can be intercepted or tampered with.

**Fix:** Serve the receiver over HTTPS and update the endpoint URL to an \`https://\` origin.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks/best-practices) · rule \`WEBHOOK_INSECURE_URL\`

#### Test/local webhook endpoint on a live account

Endpoint https://localhost:4000/hook targets a local or tunnel host (e.g. localhost / ngrok / *.test) on a live account. Live events are being routed to a development target — they will be lost in production.

**Fix:** Point live endpoints at a production HTTPS origin. Use test-mode keys and local tunnels for development, never the live account.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks/best-practices) · rule \`WEBHOOK_TEST_ENDPOINT_IN_LIVE\`

#### Customer portal does not allow payment method updates

The default portal disables payment-method updates, so customers with an expiring or failing card cannot fix it themselves — a leading cause of involuntary churn.

**Fix:** Enable "Customer can update their payment methods" on the default portal configuration.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/customer-management) · rule \`PORTAL_PAYMENT_UPDATE_DISABLED\`

#### Active product has no active price

Product prodC (prodC) is active but every one of its prices is inactive. The product cannot be purchased — any checkout or payment link for it fails.

_Impact: An active, unsellable product silently blocks every purchase attempt for it._

**Fix:** Activate a price for the product, or archive the product if it is no longer sold.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/products-prices/manage-prices) · rule \`ALL_PRICES_INACTIVE\`

#### Live-mode key is seeing test data

The audit key has a live prefix but the account responses report livemode=false. The key/environment is mismatched — results will not reflect your production configuration.

**Fix:** Use a key whose prefix matches the environment you intend to audit.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/keys) · rule \`LIVE_KEY_DETECTED_TEST\`

#### Account is not able to create charges

The account reports it cannot currently create charges. Every payment attempt will fail until this is resolved — usually an onboarding or verification gap.

_Impact: While charges are disabled, 100% of payment attempts fail._

**Fix:** Complete account onboarding / verification in the Stripe Dashboard so charges are enabled.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/api/accounts) · rule \`ACCOUNT_CHARGES_DISABLED\`

#### Account has outstanding requirements

Stripe reports outstanding account requirements (currently due: business_profile.url). Unmet requirements can disable charges or payouts, interrupting revenue.

**Fix:** Submit the outstanding requirements in the Stripe Dashboard before they disable the account.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/api/accounts) · rule \`ACCOUNT_REQUIREMENTS_DUE\`

### 🟡 Medium (15)

#### Unusually many enabled webhook endpoints (12)

12 enabled webhook endpoints exceed the advisory threshold of 10. A sprawl of endpoints is hard to keep version-consistent and is often a sign of stale test/integration endpoints left in place.

**Fix:** Audit the endpoint list and remove any that are no longer needed. Set \`options.threshold\` to tune this advisory for your integration.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/api/webhook_endpoints) · rule \`WEBHOOK_TOO_MANY_ENDPOINTS\`

#### Duplicate webhook endpoint URL

2 enabled endpoints deliver to the same URL https://dup.example.com/hook. Duplicate endpoints double-deliver every event and usually mean a forgotten copy is still live.

**Fix:** Consolidate to a single endpoint per URL and delete the duplicates, or give each a distinct URL if duplication is intentional.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/api/webhook_endpoints) · rule \`WEBHOOK_DUPLICATE_URL\`

#### Webhook endpoint pinned to an outdated API version

Endpoint https://old.example.com/hook is pinned to API version 2024-09-30.acacia, older than the latest Stripe release the audit knows (2026-06-24.dahlia). Pinned endpoints receive event shapes from an older release, which drift from newer integration code.

**Fix:** Upgrade the endpoint to the latest API version after confirming your handler tolerates the newer event shapes, or intentionally re-pin it.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/upgrades) · rule \`WEBHOOK_API_VERSION_MISMATCH\`

#### Customer portal does not offer self-serve cancellation

The default portal disables subscription cancellation. Forcing cancellations through support frustrates customers and risks chargebacks / disputes.

**Fix:** Enable "Customer can cancel subscriptions" on the default portal configuration.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/customer-management) · rule \`PORTAL_NO_CANCEL_FLOW\`

#### Customer portal does not show invoice history

The default portal hides invoice history, so customers cannot self-serve receipts and billing records — a recurring support burden.

**Fix:** Enable "Customer can view their invoice history" on the default portal configuration.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/customer-management) · rule \`PORTAL_NO_INVOICE_HISTORY\`

#### Customer portal does not allow customer detail updates

The default portal disables customer-detail updates (email, address, tax ID), so customers cannot keep their billing information current.

**Fix:** Enable "Customer can update their information" on the default portal configuration.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/customer-management) · rule \`PORTAL_NO_CUSTOMER_UPDATE\`

#### Portal plan changes apply no proration

The default portal allows subscription updates but sets proration to 'none'. Mid-cycle upgrades are not charged the prorated difference, leaking revenue on every in-portal plan change.

**Fix:** Set the portal's subscription-update proration behavior to 'create_prorations' (or 'always_invoice') so upgrades are billed correctly.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/customer-management) · rule \`PORTAL_PRORATION_NONE_ON_UPDATE\`

#### Active price is set to zero

Price priceB1 is active with a unit amount of 0 USD. If this is not an intentional free tier, the product is being given away.

**Fix:** Confirm the zero amount is intentional; otherwise set a non-zero unit amount.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/products-prices/manage-prices) · rule \`PRICE_ZERO_AMOUNT\`

#### Product default price missing or inactive

Product prodA (prodA) has no default price set. Stripe requires the default price to be an active price; payment links and Checkout that rely on it will misbehave.

**Fix:** Set the product default price to an active price.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/products-prices/manage-prices) · rule \`DEFAULT_PRICE_MISSING_OR_INACTIVE\`

#### Product default price missing or inactive

Product prodC (prodC) points at inactive price priceC1. Stripe requires the default price to be an active price; payment links and Checkout that rely on it will misbehave.

**Fix:** Set the product default price to an active price.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/products-prices/manage-prices) · rule \`DEFAULT_PRICE_MISSING_OR_INACTIVE\`

#### Active price has unspecified tax behavior

Price priceA1 is active with tax_behavior unspecified. Stripe cannot tell whether the amount is tax-inclusive or exclusive, risking incorrect tax on every charge.

**Fix:** Set the price's tax_behavior to 'inclusive' or 'exclusive'.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/tax) · rule \`PRICE_TAX_BEHAVIOR_UNSPECIFIED\`

#### Customer-chosen price has no minimum

Price priceD1 lets the customer choose the amount (custom_unit_amount) but sets no minimum. Customers can pay arbitrarily little — including effectively nothing.

**Fix:** Set a minimum on the custom unit amount so pay-what-you-want has a floor.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/products-prices/manage-prices) · rule \`CUSTOM_UNIT_AMOUNT_NO_MINIMUM\`

#### No default tax behavior configured

Stripe Tax is set up but has no default tax behavior (inclusive/exclusive). Prices without an explicit tax behavior fall back to ambiguous handling.

**Fix:** Set a default tax behavior ('inclusive' or 'exclusive') in your Stripe Tax settings.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/tax/set-up) · rule \`DEFAULT_TAX_BEHAVIOR_UNSET\`

#### Stripe Tax onboarding is incomplete

Stripe Tax status is pending — onboarding (origin address, default tax code) is unfinished, so automatic tax is not fully active.

**Fix:** Finish Stripe Tax onboarding (origin address + default product tax code) to move status to active.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/tax/set-up) · rule \`TAX_SETTINGS_PENDING\`

#### No statement descriptor configured

No statement descriptor is set, so charges may appear on customer card statements without a recognizable name — a leading cause of "I don’t recognize this charge" disputes and chargebacks.

**Fix:** Set a clear statement descriptor in the Stripe Dashboard (Settings → Public details / Payments).

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/api/accounts) · rule \`STATEMENT_DESCRIPTOR_MISSING\`

### 🔵 Low (6)

#### Customer portal hosted login page is disabled

The default portal has the hosted login page off, so there is no shareable self-serve URL — every portal visit must be deep-linked from your app.

**Fix:** Enable the hosted login page on the default portal configuration if you want a standalone portal URL.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/customer-management) · rule \`PORTAL_LOGIN_PAGE_DISABLED\`

#### Active recurring price has no lookup key

Price priceA1 is active and recurring but has no lookup_key. Referencing prices by raw id is brittle — a lookup_key lets you swap the underlying price without a code change.

**Fix:** Assign a stable lookup_key to the price so your integration references it by key, not id.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/products-prices/manage-prices) · rule \`PRICE_NO_LOOKUP_KEY\`

#### Multiple active prices for the same product and cadence

Product prodA has more than one active price for the same currency, billing cadence, and amount shape. Stripe's convention is exactly one canonical active price per cadence; duplicates make "the price" ambiguous.

**Fix:** Archive the redundant prices, keeping one canonical active price per currency/cadence/amount (grandfathered prices aside).

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/products-prices/manage-prices) · rule \`MULTIPLE_ACTIVE_PRICES_PER_PRODUCT\`

#### Product mixes multi-currency mechanisms

Product prodE has active prices in multiple base currencies AND prices using currency_options. Mixing per-currency Price objects with on-price currency_options makes "which price applies" ambiguous and easy to misconfigure.

**Fix:** Pick one multi-currency mechanism per product — either separate Price objects per currency, or a single price with currency_options.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/products-prices/manage-prices) · rule \`CROSS_CURRENCY_PRICES\`

#### Account branding (icon/logo) not configured

The account has neither a branding icon nor a logo, so receipts, invoices, and the customer portal render unbranded — eroding trust and increasing dispute risk.

**Fix:** Upload a branding icon and/or logo in the Stripe Dashboard (Settings → Branding).

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/api/accounts) · rule \`UNBRANDED_RECEIPTS\`

#### No default account tax IDs configured for invoices

No default account tax IDs are set, so your business tax registration number does not appear on invoices — a compliance gap in many jurisdictions.

**Fix:** Set default account tax IDs in your invoice settings so they appear on every invoice.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/api/accounts) · rule \`DEFAULT_ACCOUNT_TAX_IDS_MISSING\`

### ⚪ Info (2)

#### v2 event destinations were not audited

This audit covers classic /v1/webhook_endpoints only. If you also use v2 event destinations ("thin events"), they are not included in this report.

**Fix:** Review v2 event destinations separately in the Stripe Dashboard if your integration uses them.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/event-destinations) · rule \`EVENT_DESTINATIONS_NOT_AUDITED\`

#### Restricted key did not grant every read scope

The audit key could not read: tax. Rules over those regions were skipped, so this audit is partial. (This is expected if you intentionally scoped the key narrower than the audit's 6 read regions.)

**Fix:** Grant the key read access to tax for a complete audit, or accept the reduced coverage.

[Stripe docs](https://docs.stripe.com/keys#create-restricted-api-secret-key) · rule \`RESTRICTED_KEY_PERMISSION_PROBE\`

